WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.269
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.269 --> 00:00:12.251
Bro Code Article 35,
a bro must never watch a romantic comedy.

00:00:12.251 --> 00:00:15.438
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Bro Code Article 8,

00:00:15.438 --> 00:00:18.758
a bro must never send
a birthday card to another bro.

00:00:18.758 --> 00:00:25.813
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Bro Code Article 41, a bro never cries.

00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:32.028
The Bro Code has 125 articles, and
I first learned about the bro code

00:00:32.028 --> 00:00:36.520
available at brocode.com from Mason,
a junior in high school.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.730
I never met Mason before, but he'd agreed
to meet with me, or more accurately,

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:45.190
he'd had his arm twisted by
his mom my former coworker.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:52.050
I've spent the past three months talking
to boys and young men like Mason.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.256
I reached out to them because I wanted to
understand what it means to be a man in

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:56.929
2020.

00:00:56.929 --> 00:01:04.038
What I found, is that our culture's
definition of masculinity is broken.

00:01:04.038 --> 00:01:07.813
It's outdated, it's flawed, and

00:01:07.813 --> 00:01:13.305
it's emotionally and
psychologically dangerous.

00:01:15.255 --> 00:01:19.310
In the past 50 years in America, we've
redefined what it means to be a woman.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.920
Young girls today are told that
they can do anything, be anyone.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:28.620
The author Ruth Whitman jokes that if
parents today were naming their children

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:33.430
after virtues like the Puritans used to
do, no girls would be named Charity or

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.510
Grace or Patience.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:40.688
Instead, half of all baby girls born
in America would be named empowerment.

00:01:40.688 --> 00:01:42.963
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or assertiveness.

00:01:42.963 --> 00:01:45.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Of course,

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.790
it's still incredibly difficult
to be a woman in 2020.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:52.272
Girls today, women today, they face
centuries of prejudices that have

00:01:52.272 --> 00:01:57.284
calcified over the years and we're just
beginning to chip away at the surfaces.

00:01:57.284 --> 00:02:01.493
But we're at least beginning to have
nuanced conversations around what it

00:02:01.493 --> 00:02:03.600
means to be a woman.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.930
Young girls today are taught that,
femininity can take many different

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:12.870
forms our boys though,
have been left behind.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:18.160
There are many interpretations
of masculinity and

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.230
millions of men subscribe
to their own definition.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:25.684
But our culture is predominant definition

00:02:25.684 --> 00:02:29.730
of masculinity equates
masculinity with machismo.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.420
We tell boys to equate
sensitivity with weakness.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.800
We tell them to man up,
to suppress their emotions.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.230
We tell them that boys don't cry,
and tell them to take it like a man.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:44.958
We put our boys in
emotional straitjackets.

00:02:47.487 --> 00:02:48.668
When I was in high school,

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:52.250
you could hardly go a day without
hearing the words no homo.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:56.470
When a guy saw another guy in the hallway
he would often maybe hug him and say no

00:02:56.470 --> 00:03:00.190
homo, just to indicate that in case anyone
was wondering he definitely isn't gay.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:05.200
Now the University of Oregon Sociologists,
CJ Pascoe,

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.240
has actually done research on no homo.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.840
Sifting through more than 1,000 different
tweets that included the phrase.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.460
And what she found is that
many of these tweets,

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.430
simply expressed a positive emotion.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.205
Something innocuous like, I love
chocolate ice cream hashtag no homo.

00:03:21.205 --> 00:03:25.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I did my own search online,

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:29.380
and I found that many tweets were from
young men to their male friends and

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:32.980
they express things like I miss you,
or let's hang out soon.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:39.420
Pascoe concluded that no homo
is a form of inoculation

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:40.810
against insults from other guys.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.280
It's a shield that allows
boys to be fully human.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.709
Now I want to be clear that this
isn't about sexual orientation.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:56.271
As gay man it's true that I've been
unusually sensitive to stereotypes around

00:03:56.271 --> 00:03:57.701
masculinity in my life.

00:03:59.704 --> 00:04:02.861
But it's also given me
a different perspective,

00:04:02.861 --> 00:04:07.230
a view that's unvarnished of our
culture expectations of manhood.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:13.076
Meanwhile I've watched straight friends,
as well as gay friends,

00:04:13.076 --> 00:04:17.566
as well as myself, suffocate under
the weight of these expectations.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.783
In infancy, there's no difference
between the sexes' need for

00:04:23.783 --> 00:04:27.840
emotional connection or
in their capacity to show emotion.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.635
There's actually some evidence that male
infants are more emotionally expressive,

00:04:31.635 --> 00:04:35.490
than females, but
soon that begins to shift.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.230
The Harvard psychologist William Pollack
notes, that by adolescence,

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:45.810
boys become shame phobic, convinced that
peers will lose respect for them or see

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:50.350
them as lesser if they talk about personal
problems or discuss their feelings.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:56.990
The author Liz Plank notes that boys have
their gender constantly surveiled for

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.760
any sign of misstep or mistake.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.480
Boeys Che says, become fluent
in emotional self-censorship.

00:05:07.208 --> 00:05:10.200
There's a George Orwell story
called Shooting An Elephant.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.100
And in the story there's a colonial
police man who's tasked with

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.630
killing an elephant running
wild in his village.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:20.560
And he's told to do it without showing
a hint of hesitation or remorse or regret.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:22.880
But he's torn apart inside.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:29.943
The sentence that Orwell uses to
describe how the police man felt,

00:05:29.943 --> 00:05:33.610
is he wears a mask and
his face grows to fit it.

00:05:36.315 --> 00:05:39.320
From a young age, our boys wear masks.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:44.254
And those masks soon become
how they view themselves,

00:05:44.254 --> 00:05:47.480
hardened, tough, unflappable.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:53.740
They become disconnected from the emotions
in their life, suffer for many intimacy.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:00.070
It's that disconnection that
leads to a loss of empathy.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:06.040
Speaking with Mason, I was stroke by how
his language went talking about girls,

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:13.544
was alluded in violence Pound,
nail, hammer.

00:06:15.847 --> 00:06:18.737
Women were dehumanized, objects,

00:06:18.737 --> 00:06:23.220
a means to claim a place in
the hierarchy of masculinity.

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:31.515
This is dangerous, this disconnection,
this loss of empathy,

00:06:31.515 --> 00:06:38.306
it leads to isolation, loneliness,
desperation, needs to men lashing out.

00:06:38.306 --> 00:06:41.190
97% of school shootings has been by man.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.490
Men are three and a half times as
likely as women to kill themselves.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:50.105
Nearly half of domestic violence or

00:06:50.105 --> 00:06:54.092
female homicides are the result
of domestic violence of men.

00:06:56.113 --> 00:06:57.390
Our boys are trapped.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:00.100
Our men are suffocating.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.736
And they can't even tell us
that they're suffocating,

00:07:04.736 --> 00:07:08.890
because we haven't allowed them the
emotional vocabulary to cry out for help.

00:07:11.777 --> 00:07:13.195
So what can we do about it?

00:07:14.947 --> 00:07:16.770
We can start with our parenting.

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:20.470
I feel so
lucky to have been raised by my dad.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:25.905
My dad and my mum passed away when I was
one, and my dad raised my brother and me

00:07:25.905 --> 00:07:31.235
on his own, and he is the most emotional
and sensitive man you'll ever meet.

00:07:31.235 --> 00:07:32.890
He cries like 12 times a day.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:35.645
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He calls me honey and

00:07:35.645 --> 00:07:39.055
my special one every
single morning growing up.

00:07:39.055 --> 00:07:41.645
My dad woke me up by
coming into my room and

00:07:41.645 --> 00:07:45.455
softly singing, here comes the sun by
The Beatles while turning on the lights.

00:07:45.455 --> 00:07:48.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My dad has shown me that men,

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:52.615
straight men, can be sensitive,
and that compassion, and

00:07:52.615 --> 00:07:56.410
vulnerability form the foundation for
the strongest relationships.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:00.000
Change starts with parents.

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:05.410
Parents can encourage boys to
talk about their emotions.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.220
And parents can talk to their
boys about their emotions too.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.710
In doing so
they can normalize emotional discourse.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:15.020
This is especially important for
fathers, and father figures.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.340
Or maybe it’s a male rule model or
school teacher.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:21.990
Men need to see emotional, reciprocal
healthy relationships with boys.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.870
That connectivity that’s
built on depth and nuance.

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:29.700
And we can stop gendering things.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:34.200
So much focus has been on asking
women to lean in to speak up,

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.752
be assertive, power pose in the bathroom
before that next big meeting.

00:08:37.752 --> 00:08:41.513
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We can also tell boys to be sensitive,

00:08:41.513 --> 00:08:44.012
to be vulnerable, to listen more.

00:08:44.012 --> 00:08:49.909
We can treat people as people first and
foremost not as men and woman.

00:08:49.909 --> 00:08:54.668
Gender today tells us how to behave
instead of letting us behave in

00:08:54.668 --> 00:08:57.270
a way that's natural to who we are.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:02.145
If something's aggressive,
we can say that it's aggressive and

00:09:02.145 --> 00:09:04.242
call it that not masculine.

00:09:04.242 --> 00:09:08.004
If something's feminine, or
if something's nurturing,

00:09:08.004 --> 00:09:12.300
we can say that it's actually
just nurturing and not feminine.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.740
These categories
are constructions on our culture.

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:19.590
There's nothing inherently more masculine
about being braver or strong, any

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:23.760
more than there's anything more inherently
feminine, about being gentle or kind.

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:26.900
We can rewrite the rules of our culture.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:32.964
The woman's movement has redefined
what it means to be a woman in 2020.

00:09:32.964 --> 00:09:36.161
It's time we redefine what
it means to be a man.

00:09:36.161 --> 00:09:37.367
Thank you.

00:09:37.367 --> 00:09:38.784
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:38.784 --> 00:09:43.772
[MUSIC]

